WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
The central quantum concern for TLS planning is that some widely used public-key

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.075
algorithms are considered vulnerable to future cryptographically relevant quantum

00:00:08.075 --> 00:00:08.850
computers.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.430
RSA, finite-field Diffie-Hellman, elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman,

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:15.475
and ECDSA appear across many web and service environments.

00:00:15.475 --> 00:00:18.875
This does not mean every TLS endpoint is instantly unsafe today.

00:00:18.875 --> 00:00:23.040
It means teams should identify where these mechanisms protect information that must remain

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.890
trustworthy or confidential over a long period.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.000
Symmetric encryption and hashing are affected differently.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.130
They are still part of quantum risk discussions,

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.360
but the migration pressure is not identical to the public-key case.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:33.805
In TLS,

00:00:33.805 --> 00:00:37.515
the public-key portion of the handshake is what makes captured traffic and future

00:00:37.515 --> 00:00:39.600
decryption scenarios especially important.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.915
The application data is protected with symmetric keys,

00:00:41.915 --> 00:00:45.380
but the way those keys were established may become the weak link for long-lived

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:46.281
confidentiality.

00:00:46.281 --> 00:00:50.220
Prioritization should consider data lifetime, confidentiality sensitivity,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.004
internet exposure, operational criticality, dependency complexity,

00:00:54.004 --> 00:00:55.941
and business or regulatory impact.

00:00:55.941 --> 00:00:59.380
Public-facing endpoints carrying sensitive data usually deserve attention.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.580
So do government and enterprise APIs, machine-to-machine services, mobile backends,

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:06.435
internal service mesh traffic with sensitive business data,

00:01:06.435 --> 00:01:09.480
and partner integrations that cross organizational boundaries.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.655
A static brochure site has a different risk profile than an API carrying regulated

00:01:13.655 --> 00:01:14.340
records.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.115
Not every endpoint has the same urgency, and that is good news.

00:01:17.115 --> 00:01:21.240
A mature program can rank systems instead of trying to change everything at once.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:26.040
Teams should identify high-value traffic, long-lived data, externally exposed services,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.360
clients that are hard to update,

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.948
and dependencies controlled by vendors or cloud providers.

00:01:29.948 --> 00:01:34.395
Prioritization turns PQC from a vague concern into a sequenced roadmap that engineering

00:01:34.395 --> 00:01:36.680
and operations teams can actually execute.
